From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: domicile CI <ci@domicile.invalid>
Date: Fri, 11 Sep 2026 09:15:00 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] domicile: let a guest's focus reach the element it hangs off

A click inside a <webview> tells the embedder's document nothing. This makes it
tell it two things: the element becomes document.activeElement, and it says so
in an event a shell can hear.

WHY THERE IS NOTHING THERE TODAY, in upstream's own words a dozen lines below
this hunk: "for cross-origin (remote) frames, DOM focus events do not cross
process boundaries to reach the frame owner element in the parent document".
A guest is exactly such a frame. The press moves focus all the way to the
embedder -- OnInputEventPreDispatch answers a kMouseDown with
FocusOwningWebContents, SetFocusedFrameTree walks out through
FocusOuterFrameTrees, and the outer delegate's proxy is told it is focused, so
FocusController::SetFocusedFrame does run in the shell's renderer with the
placeholder RemoteFrame -- and FocusDocumentView has already CLEARED the
embedder document's focused element on the way in. The element ends up neither
focused nor activeElement, and no focus event is dispatched on it.

WHAT THAT COSTS A SHELL. Clicking a window raises it, in every desktop there
is. Domicile's shells raise a client's window on `focus_changed` from the
compositor; a browser window's page never moves the seat, because that page is
inside the chrome's own window, and no pointer event inside a guest crosses
back out either. The focus was the last thing left to cross, and it does not:
clicking into a browser window left it under whatever was covering it, with
the rail still highlighting the window before it.

THE FOCUS IS UPSTREAM'S OWN SHAPE, three lines above. A fenced frame has the
same problem -- a page in another frame tree, reached through a placeholder
remote frame -- and gets the same treatment, for the reason its comment gives:
"this will allow methods like document.activeElement and document.hasFocus()
to properly handle when a fenced frame has focus". A <webview> is the fork's
other such owner, so it is the fork's other such case. The re-entrancy is
handled the way that branch's comment says it is: SetFocusedElement calls back
into this function, `is_changing_focused_frame_` is already true, so the call
returns early and the rest of this invocation still runs. The same is true of
the callback through HTMLFrameElementBase::SetFocused, which asks for the
content frame to be focused -- which is where focus already is.

AND THE ELEMENT SAYS SO ITSELF, WHICH IS THE HALF A FIRST ATTEMPT MISSED AND
THE GUARD CAUGHT. Focusing the element buys document.activeElement and nothing
else: Document::SetFocusedElement dispatches `focus` and `focusin` only inside
`if (GetPage() && GetPage()->GetFocusController().IsFocused())`, whose comment
is "if page lost focus, event will be dispatched on page focus, don't
duplicate" -- and the embedder's page has already lost focus by the time this
runs, because WebContentsImpl::SetFocusedFrameTree sends the old tree's widget
SetPageFocus(false) BEFORE it calls FocusOuterFrameTrees. A guest taking focus
is precisely the case those events are suppressed in. guard-webview-click.sh
measured exactly that: `activeElement=webview hasFocus=false` in the embedder's
document, with no event of any kind beside it.

So HTMLWebViewElement overrides SetFocused, which Document::SetFocusedElement
calls on whatever it focuses, and dispatches an event that is not a focus event
and is therefore not suppressed. Hanging the announcement there rather than on
a call from this function is the same measurement read twice: an element that
is activeElement has had SetFocused run, whichever route the focus came by,
where a call from this branch fires only if this branch is that route.

THE EDIT TO html_frame_element_base.h IS WHAT LETS IT, and it is a move rather
than the one-word change it looks like it should be. SetFocused was private and
`final`; it is protected and `override` now. A subclass may override a private
virtual and may NOT call one, and the base's own work -- handing the content
frame the focus -- still has to happen on every focus the element takes. The
first attempt changed only `final`, and the compiler said so.

MEASURED BY guard-webview-click.sh, which clicks in a running engine's browser
window and reads what reaches the shell's document, with a control that clicks
the shell's own chrome instead. Nothing below a real engine can make this
claim: there is no nested browsing context in happy-dom, so the shell's unit
test passes on a synthesised event whether or not a real one ever arrives --
which is how the shell shipped a handler for an event nothing sent.

See packages/shell-manganese/src/BrowserWindow.tsx in the Domicile repository
for the other end of it.
---
 .../core/html/html_frame_element_base.h       |  7 ++++-
 .../renderer/core/page/focus_controller.cc    | 58 ++++++++++++++++++++
 2 files changed, 64 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/third_party/blink/renderer/core/html/html_frame_element_base.h b/third_party/blink/renderer/core/html/html_frame_element_base.h
index 8828fbd..8f3eeb5 100644
--- a/third_party/blink/renderer/core/html/html_frame_element_base.h
+++ b/third_party/blink/renderer/core/html/html_frame_element_base.h
@@ -60,6 +60,12 @@ class CORE_EXPORT HTMLFrameElementBase : public HTMLFrameOwnerElement {
   void DidNotifySubtreeInsertionsToDocument() override;
   void AttachLayoutTree(AttachContext&) override;
 
+  // Domicile's engine fork: protected and `override` rather than private and
+  // `final`, so that <webview> can hear its own focus and still hand the frame
+  // the focus this does. A subclass may override a private virtual; it may not
+  // call one. See core/html/domicile/html_web_view_element.h.
+  void SetFocused(bool, mojom::blink::FocusType) override;
+
   void SetScrollbarMode(mojom::blink::ScrollbarMode);
   void SetMarginWidth(int);
   void SetMarginHeight(int);
@@ -78,7 +84,6 @@ class CORE_EXPORT HTMLFrameElementBase : public HTMLFrameOwnerElement {
     return FocusableState::kFocusable;
   }
   int DefaultTabIndex() const final;
-  void SetFocused(bool, mojom::blink::FocusType) final;
 
   bool IsURLAttribute(const Attribute&) const final;
   bool HasLegalLinkAttribute(const QualifiedName&) const final;
diff --git a/third_party/blink/renderer/core/page/focus_controller.cc b/third_party/blink/renderer/core/page/focus_controller.cc
index 864c2c8..33bb6bf 100644
--- a/third_party/blink/renderer/core/page/focus_controller.cc
+++ b/third_party/blink/renderer/core/page/focus_controller.cc
@@ -31,6 +31,7 @@
 #include <ranges>
 
 #include "base/containers/adapters.h"
+#include "base/logging.h"
 #include "base/memory/stack_allocated.h"
 #include "third_party/blink/public/mojom/input/focus_type.mojom-blink.h"
 #include "third_party/blink/renderer/core/accessibility/ax_object_cache.h"
@@ -57,6 +58,7 @@
 #include "third_party/blink/renderer/core/frame/local_frame_view.h"
 #include "third_party/blink/renderer/core/frame/remote_frame.h"
 #include "third_party/blink/renderer/core/frame/settings.h"
+#include "third_party/blink/renderer/core/html/domicile/html_web_view_element.h"
 #include "third_party/blink/renderer/core/html/fenced_frame/html_fenced_frame_element.h"
 #include "third_party/blink/renderer/core/html/forms/html_form_control_element.h"
 #include "third_party/blink/renderer/core/html/forms/html_form_element.h"
@@ -1803,6 +1805,62 @@ void FocusController::SetFocusedFrame(Frame* frame, bool notify_embedder) {
       // invocation of the function to run.
       SetFocusedElement(fenced_frame, frame);
     }
+
+    // Domicile's engine fork: a <webview>'s guest is the same shape of thing
+    // -- a page in another frame tree, reached through a placeholder remote
+    // frame -- and it wants the same answer, for the reason the comment below
+    // gives about remote frames generally: DOM focus events do not cross the
+    // process boundary to the owner, so without this nothing in the embedder's
+    // document knows that the user clicked into the page. A shell raises the
+    // window that was clicked, and a browser window is the only kind whose
+    // pointer events it never sees at all.
+    //
+    // THE FOCUS IS ALL THIS BUYS, and the element carries the rest.
+    // Document::SetFocusedElement dispatches `focus` and `focusin` only while
+    // the page is focused, and the embedder's page has ALREADY lost focus by
+    // the time this runs: WebContentsImpl::SetFocusedFrameTree sends the old
+    // tree's widget SetPageFocus(false) before FocusOuterFrameTrees reaches
+    // this renderer. Measured, not reasoned -- guard-webview-click.sh read
+    // `activeElement=webview` out of the embedder with no event beside it. So
+    // HTMLWebViewElement::SetFocused, which this reaches through
+    // Document::SetFocusedElement, is where the element says so itself. The
+    // move of HTMLFrameElementBase::SetFocused into `protected` is what lets
+    // it: a subclass may override a private virtual but cannot call it.
+    //
+    // THE TAG RATHER THAN THE TYPE, and that is a measurement rather than a
+    // preference. Engine run 194 read this out of a real click:
+    //
+    //   domicile: focus reached a frame owned by <webview>, webview=0
+    //
+    // The owner IS the element -- it is an HTMLFrameOwnerElement whose tag is
+    // `webview`, and an HTMLUnknownElement is not a frame owner, so nothing
+    // else it could be -- and DynamicTo<HTMLWebViewElement> said no anyway.
+    // The generated DowncastTraits works for <app> (see
+    // core/html/domicile/layout_app_surface.cc, which casts that way and is
+    // measured by the resize guard), so something about this tag's generated
+    // traits is wrong, and that is worth chasing on its own. It is not worth
+    // blocking on: the cast is not needed here. What this branch has to know
+    // is whether the owner is a <webview> rather than an ordinary <iframe>,
+    // because focusing an <iframe>'s owner would change upstream's behaviour
+    // for every frame; the tag answers exactly that. SetFocusedElement takes
+    // an Element*, and the announcement is a virtual call on whatever the
+    // element really is, so no HTMLWebViewElement* is wanted anywhere.
+    //
+    // The cast stays in the log, and only in the log, so the anomaly is on the
+    // record rather than silently worked around.
+    auto* owner = To<HTMLFrameOwnerElement>(frame->Owner());
+    const bool is_web_view = owner->HasTagName(html_names::kWebviewTag);
+    LOG(INFO) << "domicile: focus reached a frame owned by <"
+              << owner->localName().Utf8() << ">, webview=" << is_web_view
+              << " cast=" << !!DynamicTo<HTMLWebViewElement>(owner);
+    if (is_web_view) {
+      // The result, which upstream's fenced-frame call above discards:
+      // Document::SetFocusedElement can refuse, and a refusal is the one way
+      // this branch can run and the element still never hear SetFocused.
+      const bool focused = SetFocusedElement(owner, frame);
+      LOG(INFO) << "domicile: focused the <webview> the guest hangs off: "
+                << focused;
+    }
   }
 
   auto* old_frame = DynamicTo<LocalFrame>(focused_frame_.Get());
